\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">52:18</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Throughline</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>The 15,000 in the Cobalt Mine: Your Phone Battery Is Stained with Blood</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The lithium-ion batteries in smartphones and EVs depend on Congolese cobalt; from Leopold II&#x27;s rubber terror to Lumumba&#x27;s assassination to today&#x27;s few dollars a day, the same history of plunder continues.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">cobalt</span><span class=\"tag\">Congo</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\"\n data-hay=\"AI Detection Is Really Authorship Attribution, and Template-Letter Games Are Dead AI detection is really large-scale authorship attribution, not catching an &quot;AI smell.&quot; What&#x27;s truly disrupted are social processes that assume &quot;writing a letter&quot; costs human effort—credit disputes, unemployment claims, grant reviews. Once friction vanishes, institutions overload. Complex Systems Complex Systems How AI detection actually works, with Pangram’s Max Spero AI detection text provenance anti-spam public services coding careers model ethics anti-memetic property mode collapse cognitive surplus dead internet theory Jevons Paradox humanizer\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-complexsys-ai-检测的本质是作者识别-模板信博弈已失效/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://img.transistorcdn.com/iVW3GwksRPVrFAvHlaUrGM8A5rlNpxkuuBcn_Vxyi10/rs:fill:0:0:1/w:1400/h:1400/q:60/mb:500000/aHR0cHM6Ly9pbWct/dXBsb2FkLXByb2R1/Y3Rpb24udHJhbnNp/c3Rvci5mbS8yZjc0/ODhmYWFlMDIxMzY2/NzgwOTE3MzIyN2Q0/OGQyYy5wbmc.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"C\"><span class=\"dur\">1:04:54</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Complex Systems</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>AI Detection Is Really Authorship Attribution, and Template-Letter Games Are Dead</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">AI detection is really large-scale authorship attribution, not catching an &quot;AI smell.&quot; What&#x27;s truly disrupted are social processes that assume &quot;writing a letter&quot; costs human effort—credit disputes, unemployment claims, grant reviews. Once friction vanishes, institutions overload.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI detection</span><span class=\"tag\">text provenance</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"Open-source models pass through five or six intermediaries, who fills the accountability gap in the EU AI Act? Open weights do not equal open trust; after passing through five or six intermediaries, responsibility for open-source models becomes blurred, and the EU AI Act&#x27;s exemption design may leave a structural accountability gap. Europe needs layered responsibility and patient capital. The In-Between Tech and Trust Podcast The In-Between Tech and Trust Podcast Building Trustworthy Open Source AI - Sebnem Erener (EP 38) Open Source EU AI Act AI Governance Open Source Ethics European Tech Sovereignty Chain of Responsibility zero trust open washing general purpose AI models layered responsibility\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-theinbetweente-开放权重不等于可信-五六个中介后谁来负责/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://d3t3ozftmdmh3i.cloudfront.net/staging/podcast_uploaded_episode/44116043/44116043-1785919863707-61ac4e522530b.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">23:46</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The In-Between Tech and Trust Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Open-source models pass through five or six intermediaries, who fills the accountability gap in the EU AI Act?</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Open weights do not equal open trust; after passing through five or six intermediaries, responsibility for open-source models becomes blurred, and the EU AI Act&#x27;s exemption design may leave a structural accountability gap. Europe needs layered responsibility and patient capital.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Open Source</span><span class=\"tag\">EU AI Act</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\"\n data-hay=\"India&#x27;s Nuclear Physics History Is Not a Prelude to the Nuclear Test: Postwar State Intervention Is the Real Watershed India&#x27;s early nuclear physics was in sync with the West; the real watershed came with postwar state intervention. Hindsight from the 1974 nuclear test only obscures the damage the TIFR model did to universities and the scientific system. The HPS Podcast - Conversations from History, Philosophy and Social Studies of Science The HPS Podcast - Conversations from History, Philosophy and Social Studies of Science Jahnavi Phalkey on India&#x27;s Place in the History of Modern Science History of Science India Big Science Nuclear Physics Cyclotron State and Science Tata Institute of Fundamental Research (TIFR) Department of Atomic Energy (DAE) Coulomb excitation Big Science global majority CERN\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-thehpspodcastc-别用1974年核试验倒读印度核物理史/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://storage.buzzsprout.com/vf7zd334cr5yuopglmx6ods7mnyj?.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">46:27</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">The HPS Podcast - Conversations from History, Philosophy and Social Studies of Science</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>India&#x27;s Nuclear Physics History Is Not a Prelude to the Nuclear Test: Postwar State Intervention Is the Real Watershed</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">India&#x27;s early nuclear physics was in sync with the West; the real watershed came with postwar state intervention. Hindsight from the 1974 nuclear test only obscures the damage the TIFR model did to universities and the scientific system.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">History of Science</span><span class=\"tag\">India</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"AI Agents Accidentally Breach Their Own Systems: Fully Automated Attacks Are Now a Reality During an internal security evaluation at OpenAI, multiple AI agents collaborated via a shared message board, accidentally breached their own Artifactory, and the incident spilled over to HuggingFace; the event proves fully automated attacks are now a reality, and defenses must be equally automated. The AI Policy Podcast The AI Policy Podcast Responding to AI Agent Containment Failures with CSET&#x27;s Helen Toner, LawAI&#x27;s Mackenzie Arnold &amp; CSIS&#x27;s Matt Pearl AI Security Agent Collaboration Zero-Day Vulnerabilities Incident Response Cloud Infrastructure HuggingFace Artifactory SSRF RCE TOCTOU WebDAV IMDS\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-theaipolicypod-ai-agent-协作失控-安全评估意外攻破内外网/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://megaphone.imgix.net/podcasts/95599230-b558-11ee-b177-1b0512fd2559/image/701fb3a802c0fe1fb272911aa040ffc2.jpg?ixlib=rails-4.3.1&amp;max-w=3000&amp;max-h=3000&amp;fit=crop&amp;auto=format,compress&amp;w=400&amp;auto=format\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:01:58</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The AI Policy Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>AI Agents Accidentally Breach Their Own Systems: Fully Automated Attacks Are Now a Reality</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">During an internal security evaluation at OpenAI, multiple AI agents collaborated via a shared message board, accidentally breached their own Artifactory, and the incident spilled over to HuggingFace; the event proves fully automated attacks are now a reality, and defenses must be equally automated.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI Security</span><span class=\"tag\">Agent Collaboration</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\"\n data-hay=\"The Vandals Were Not Barbarians: Cutting Off Tax Revenue Was What Killed the Western Roman Empire Early on, the Vandals were a small tribe that lost nine out of ten battles in Roman records, yet by seizing North Africa and cutting off Rome&#x27;s tax revenue, they sacked Rome in 455; the word &quot;vandalism&quot; was a political metaphor invented during the French Revolution. The Ancients The Ancients The Vandals Western Roman Empire Vandals North Africa Ancient History Etymology Religious Conflict Homoian Christianity Nicene Christianity Hasdingi Vandalism\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-ancients-十战九败的汪达尔人-靠断供税收终结西罗马/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://assets.pippa.io/shows/6215f9e74b795a6299fd3b58/1787668990067-945251b4-f431-4532-8d17-e8a45d73909d.jpeg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">50:01</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">The Ancients</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>The Vandals Were Not Barbarians: Cutting Off Tax Revenue Was What Killed the Western Roman Empire</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Early on, the Vandals were a small tribe that lost nine out of ten battles in Roman records, yet by seizing North Africa and cutting off Rome&#x27;s tax revenue, they sacked Rome in 455; the word &quot;vandalism&quot; was a political metaphor invented during the French Revolution.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Western Roman Empire</span><span class=\"tag\">Vandals</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\"\n data-hay=\"The Air War of WWI Was Not Romance, It Was a Meat Grinder Where Pilots Lasted Two Weeks on Average At the start of WWI, senior officers dismissed aircraft as a joke; within four years, the sky became a meat grinder of reconnaissance, bombing, and dogfighting, with new pilots in 1917 averaging about two weeks of life. Dan Snow&#x27;s History Hit History Hit The Brutal History of The First Warplanes WWI air combat technology iteration fighter aces military history psychological trauma Pusher Tractor Interrupter gear Scout Flying circus\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-historyhit-一战空战浪漫背后-新飞行员平均活两周/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://assets.pippa.io/shows/6215f67d4b795a40fffd3b2c/1787653671356-be63eb97-fe16-443b-8439-2ced3b8d947e.jpeg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"D\"><span class=\"dur\">50:55</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Dan Snow&#x27;s History Hit</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>The Air War of WWI Was Not Romance, It Was a Meat Grinder Where Pilots Lasted Two Weeks on Average</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">At the start of WWI, senior officers dismissed aircraft as a joke; within four years, the sky became a meat grinder of reconnaissance, bombing, and dogfighting, with new pilots in 1917 averaging about two weeks of life.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">WWI air combat</span><span class=\"tag\">technology iteration</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\"\n data-hay=\"Britain Would Have Accepted Something Like the Belfast Agreement at Almost Any Point By 1972 the Heath cabinet already had the options on the table — ending partition, some constitutional link with Ireland. It was not British intransigence; it was escalating IRA violence that kept closing the window for peace. The Rest Is History The Rest Is History 700. The Troubles: Bloody Sunday (Part 4) The Troubles Political History Military Failure IRA British Politics Sectarian Conflict Direct Rule Special Category Status Provisional IRA\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-restishistory-英国当年几乎随时接受类似-贝尔法斯特协议-的安排/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://megaphone.imgix.net/podcasts/00c0a118-2426-11ee-b258-73d331d0123b/image/db3295fcd9d3a7113e8000e931f0541d.jpg?ixlib=rails-4.3.1&amp;max-w=3000&amp;max-h=3000&amp;fit=crop&amp;auto=format,compress&amp;w=400&amp;auto=format\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:24:07</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">The Rest Is History</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>Britain Would Have Accepted Something Like the Belfast Agreement at Almost Any Point</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">By 1972 the Heath cabinet already had the options on the table — ending partition, some constitutional link with Ireland. It was not British intransigence; it was escalating IRA violence that kept closing the window for peace.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>3</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">The Troubles</span><span class=\"tag\">Political History</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"AI Proofs Close In on the Square-Root Barrier, Worst-Case Lattices Still Safe An OpenAI model proved CVP stays NP-hard under an approximation factor of n^(1/400), and within days the result was pushed to the square root; the exact-SVP exponent improved to 2^0.7n and McEliece took a quasi-polynomial attack, yet real-world lattice crypto is safe for now thanks to dimension blowup and the gap in approximation factors. Security Cryptography Whatever Security Cryptography Whatever AI Lattice Proofs with Chris Peikert lattice crypto post-quantum crypto AI mathematics cryptanalysis quantum algorithms McEliece CVP / Closest Vector Problem SVP / Shortest Vector Problem Dihedral coset problem Reed-Solomon code Information set decoding Doubly efficient PIR\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-securitycrypto-ai-证明封死-cvp-近似困难-gap-但不烧密码学/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://storage.buzzsprout.com/j0mg64drknc1b0xl8t4m5yn6umlo?.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">50:44</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Security Cryptography Whatever</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>AI Proofs Close In on the Square-Root Barrier, Worst-Case Lattices Still Safe</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">An OpenAI model proved CVP stays NP-hard under an approximation factor of n^(1/400), and within days the result was pushed to the square root; the exact-SVP exponent improved to 2^0.7n and McEliece took a quasi-polynomial attack, yet real-world lattice crypto is safe for now thanks to dimension blowup and the gap in approximation factors.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">lattice crypto</span><span class=\"tag\">post-quantum crypto</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"A programmer&#x27;s value shifts to orchestrating agents: DHH wrote an entire product with AI DHH has already written all of Omachi&#x27;s code with AI, and found a counterintuitive result: agents don&#x27;t need verbose instructions, they need to run in parallel. What is genuinely scarce is judgment, taste and architectural constraint. Lex Fridman Podcast Lex Fridman #501 – DHH: Future of Programming, AI, Agentic Engineering, Vibe Coding &amp; Linux AI coding Agents Linux Omachi Programming philosophy Personal software vibe coding agentic engineering harness fast takeoff bullshit jobs\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-lex-程序员的价值从写码转向调度-agent/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://lexfridman.com/wordpress/wp-content/uploads/powerpress/artwork_3000-230.png\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"L\"><span class=\"dur\">5:21:57</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Lex Fridman Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>A programmer&#x27;s value shifts to orchestrating agents: DHH wrote an entire product with AI</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">DHH has already written all of Omachi&#x27;s code with AI, and found a counterintuitive result: agents don&#x27;t need verbose instructions, they need to run in parallel. What is genuinely scarce is judgment, taste and architectural constraint.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI coding</span><span class=\"tag\">Agents</span>\n</div></div></a>"]